# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows honours the global core.autocrlf=true and writes CRLF into the working
# tree on clone and on every branch switch. Prettier's endOfLine default is "lf", so `pnpm check`
# then fails format:check on every text file in the repository — 138 of them — for carriage returns
# alone, with no content difference at all. A fresh clone could not pass its own check.
#
# It also matters beyond formatting: the hosted deployment copies files to an aarch64 Linux host, and
# a CRLF shebang fails there with `bash\r: No such file or directory`.
#
# This changes checkout behaviour only. The repository already stores LF, because autocrlf normalised
# on the way in, so adding this produces no content change.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files Git must never touch. None are tracked today; these are here so that adding one later
# does not corrupt it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
